# Copy to .env.local for development, and set the same names on the host.
# The site renders without any of these — only selling the bridge-agent
# repository needs them, and every route says which one is missing rather than
# answering 500.

# ── Stripe ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Secret key. sk_test_… while you are testing; the success page shows a
# "test mode" badge on its own so a test purchase cannot be mistaken for a sale.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# Signing secret for /api/stripe/webhook, from the endpoint you register in the
# Stripe dashboard. Without it the webhook refuses every call: an endpoint that
# grants repository access on an unverified request is an open door.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ── Granting access ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A GitHub token that may add collaborators to the repository named in
# lib/course-product.ts.
#
# Use a FINE-GRAINED personal access token, scoped to that ONE repository, with
# Repository permissions -> Administration: Read and write (adding a
# collaborator is an admin operation). Nothing else — not "repo", which is every
# repository you can reach.
#
# Then the worst a leaked token can do is invite strangers to read one
# repository you were selling anyway. A classic token with the `repo` scope
# could push to all of them.
GITHUB_TOKEN=
# Set to 1 to log the invitation instead of sending it. For walking the flow
# without emailing a stranger.
GITHUB_GRANT_DRY_RUN=

# ── Where this deployment lives ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used to build Checkout's success and cancel URLs. Left empty, the request's
# own host is used, which is right on Vercel and right locally.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=
